| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 2 | 250 | full, 5 | 3.0 | -0.006 | 0.164 |
| 2 | 1000 | full, 5 | 3.0 | -0.012 | 0.070 |
| 2 | 4000 | full, 5 | 3.0 | -0.000 | 0.035 |
| 10 | 250 | full, 5 | 7.0 | -0.305 | 0.948 |
| 10 | 1000 | full, 5 | 7.0 | -0.031 | 0.433 |
| 10 | 4000 | full, 5 | 7.0 | +0.009 | 0.236 |
| 25 | 250 | full, 5 | 10.0 | -3.382 | 3.764 |
| 25 | 1000 | full, 5 | 11.0 | -0.451 | 2.131 |
| 25 | 4000 | full, 5 | 11.0 | +0.396 | 1.452 |
| 25 | 1000 | full, 25 | 11.0 | +0.731 | 0.311 |
| 25 | 4000 | full, 25 | 11.0 | +0.179 | 0.185 |
| 25 | 1000 | diagonal, 25 | 11.1 | +0.024 | 0.332 |
| 25 | 4000 | diagonal, 25 | 11.0 | -0.009 | 0.162 |

Mass on the smaller mode (true 0.300), mean of 20 runs: SMC 0.296, random-walk Metropolis 0.000.
\image(figures/smc.svg)
// result_end

In two dimensions, three stages are enough and the evidence is accurate to a few hundredths with 1000 particles. The adaptive schedule lengthens with dimension (3, 7 and 11 stages), because the conditional ESS criterion limits how much new information each step may add, and the likelihood carries more information when there are more parameters. The schedules in the figure start with very small steps, where the prior and the likelihood disagree most, and grow as the particles concentrate.

Precision depends on the moves. With only five random-walk sweeps per stage in 25 dimensions, the particles do not reach equilibrium between stages. The SD of \(\log\hat Z\) is then 1.5 even with 4000 particles, and with few particles \(\log\hat Z\) is badly biased downwards. That is expected: \(\hat Z\) is unbiased, so a noisy estimate is biased downwards on the log scale. Twenty-five sweeps reduce the SD to 0.19. They also reveal a second effect, a positive bias of 0.73 at \(N=1000\) that shrinks roughly as \(1/N\). It comes from estimating the full \(25\times25\) proposal covariance from the same particles that are then moved. Each particle's kernel depends weakly on its own position, so the population is not exactly invariant. Adapting only the 25 variances (`SmcProposal::diagonal`) removes it: the bias is +0.02 and −0.01, within Monte Carlo error. In this experiment the bias was located by checking that a fixed, population-independent proposal gave no bias, and that it shrank with \(N\) but not with a fixed temperature schedule.

The bimodal example shows why SMC is used even when the evidence is not needed. A random-walk chain started in the larger mode never crosses to the other, and puts no mass on a mode that holds 30% of the posterior. SMC starts from prior draws that cover both modes, and tempering moves the mass between them through the weights rather than through the moves. Its average estimate of 0.296 is within Monte Carlo error of 0.300.

>> Practical guidance

- Supply a normalised log prior if the evidence is needed. Only the likelihood is tempered, so the prior's constant enters the estimate.
- Keep the conditional ESS target between 0.3 and 0.9. Higher values give more stages and lower variance at proportionally higher cost.
- Increase the number of move sweeps until \(\log\hat Z\) and the posterior summaries stop changing, and check the per-stage acceptance rates in `stages`.
- For evidence in more than about ten dimensions, prefer the diagonal proposal or many more particles than parameters squared. Use the full covariance when strong correlation makes diagonal moves too slow.
- Run SMC several times with different seeds and report the spread of \(\log\hat Z\) as its Monte Carlo error. A single run carries no internal error estimate.

>> References

- P. Del Moral, A. Doucet and A. Jasra (2006). Sequential Monte Carlo samplers. *Journal of the Royal Statistical Society B* 68(3), 411-436. doi:10.1111/j.1467-9868.2006.00553.x
- N. Chopin (2002). A sequential particle filter method for static models. *Biometrika* 89(3), 539-552. doi:10.1093/biomet/89.3.539
- Y. Zhou, A. M. Johansen and J. A. D. Aston (2016). Toward automatic model comparison: an adaptive sequential Monte Carlo approach. *Journal of Computational and Graphical Statistics* 25(3), 701-726. doi:10.1080/10618600.2015.1060885
- A. Beskos, A. Jasra, N. Kantas and A. Thiery (2016). On the convergence of adaptive sequential Monte Carlo methods. *Annals of Applied Probability* 26(2), 1111-1146. doi:10.1214/15-AAP1113
- N. Chopin and O. Papaspiliopoulos (2020). *An Introduction to Sequential Monte Carlo*. Springer.
